<< /A 79 0 R /Alt (Flowchart illustrates the pathophysiology of diabetic nephropathy, detailing metabolic, oxidative stress, and hemodynamic pathways, key molecular events, and feedback mechanisms that lead to decreased glomerular filtration rate and kidney damage in diabetes.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 35 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
19 0 obj
<< /A 80 0 R /Alt (Flowchart illustrating the molecular pathways and effects of various phytochemicals in preventing diabetic nephropathy, detailing the roles of compounds like cinnamic acid, quercetin, naringin, and luteolin in regulating glucose, oxidative stress, inflammation, and apoptosis, supported by increases or decreases in relevant biomarkers and enzyme activities.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 52 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
20 0 obj
<< /A 81 0 R /Alt (Circular infographic titled \215Impacts of Alternative Therapies on Diabetic Nephropathy\216 organized into six domains: oxidative stress reduction and antioxidant enhancement, enhancement of renal function, anti-inflammatory and immunomodulatory effects, histopathological improvements, anti-fibrotic and tissue protective actions, and regulation of lipid and glucose metabolism. Each segment details specific upregulated and downregulated biomarkers or effects, such as increased antioxidant gene activity, reduced pro-inflammatory cytokines, improved renal morphology, reduced lipid peroxidation, and decreased insulin resistance. Color-coded arrows indicate increases or decreases for each parameter within the therapy domains.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 67 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
